WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:08.350
So good morning.

00:00:08.350 --> 00:00:09.350
Good morning.

00:00:09.350 --> 00:00:11.019
All things small and consequential.

00:00:11.019 --> 00:00:18.320
We're going to start with Paul because editing
the journal and you float among all these

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:19.320
fields.

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:25.100
So I wonder if it would be good for you to
give us a sort of a cosmic overview of this

00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:26.670
technology and these sciences.

00:00:26.670 --> 00:00:33.960
Really the field comes in from two directions
one is building from the bottom up like chemistry.

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:40.870
Where we attach atoms and molecules together
and then try and often make something functional

00:00:40.870 --> 00:00:42.710
or something with new properties.

00:00:42.710 --> 00:00:50.350
And then also sculpting from the, from the
top down much as we've done for centuries.

00:00:50.350 --> 00:00:56.809
But in this case we use materials like silicon
where we'll cut it up in little pieces and

00:00:56.809 --> 00:01:03.230
we'll turn some of the parts into insulators
and some into metals and some into functional

00:01:03.230 --> 00:01:08.340
semiconductors that make a billion devices
on a chip that have enabled our computers

00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:09.370
and phones and so forth.

00:01:09.370 --> 00:01:15.210
Can you give just a quick description of the
difference between gold at the scale of someone's

00:01:15.210 --> 00:01:19.210
watch and gold at the scale of an atom and
why it's so different?

00:01:19.210 --> 00:01:25.939
Right so the properties change as one goes
from atoms all the way up to the to macroscopic

00:01:25.939 --> 00:01:30.170
objects and in fact gold nanoparticles have
been used for centuries.

00:01:30.170 --> 00:01:36.520
I think Vicki often shows in her talks the
Lycurgus Cup from Roman times were gold appears

00:01:36.520 --> 00:01:41.350
red in stained glass and some of you will
know Rose glass.

00:01:41.350 --> 00:01:43.500
So I have some mid 19th century glasses.

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:49.469
My wife got me as a birthday present and the
red color comes from gold nanoparticles that

00:01:49.469 --> 00:01:50.909
were put in the glass.

00:01:50.909 --> 00:01:58.109
So even, even long ago there were nanoparticles
they just weren't called that at the time.

00:01:58.109 --> 00:02:05.380
Speaking of Vicki, so how did they get from
an application like, well in glass or whatever

00:02:05.380 --> 00:02:07.950
you know modern application into the environment
around us?

00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:11.710
Well actually that depends a lot on how it's
used so if something is you know in a piece

00:02:11.710 --> 00:02:14.960
of glass you have to wait for the glass to
break down and in that case it's a problem

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:17.550
for many many generations from now.

00:02:17.550 --> 00:02:21.700
But if you look at other applications, sunscreen
is a good one, they're actually held in an

00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:24.191
emulsion that we rub on our skin and so they're
right there.

00:02:24.191 --> 00:02:28.860
And in fact they're designed to coat the skin
so they're there in a large quantity.

00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:33.500
So I think what's interesting is there's so
many different uses of nanomaterials, consumers,

00:02:33.500 --> 00:02:39.570
businesses, in our devices and in device case,
or you know the glass you drink case it's

00:02:39.570 --> 00:02:42.770
going to be in a landfill somewhere where
we're eventually going to see it or maybe

00:02:42.770 --> 00:02:44.180
in an incinerator.

00:02:44.180 --> 00:02:48.220
In the case of personal care products we could
be, you know, exposed to it every day.

00:02:48.220 --> 00:02:53.010
So I think that's kind of the range of different
ways that nanoparticles can find themselves

00:02:53.010 --> 00:02:56.290
interacting with us or interacting with other
species in the environment.

00:02:56.290 --> 00:03:01.020
And what kind of work is required to sort
of clarify how they move and we'll get to

00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:02.770
the risk question in a minute, but…

00:03:02.770 --> 00:03:05.150
This is the exposure questions like, where
are they?

00:03:05.150 --> 00:03:09.050
Well, so it's an interesting set of work so
they're really really tiny.

00:03:09.050 --> 00:03:12.780
I'll add to this sort of how big are they
so I had this okay we're in Manhattan right

00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:19.940
so if Manhattan was a human hair a, ten nanometre,
which is kind of bang on the middle of nano

00:03:19.940 --> 00:03:23.440
materials would be about the size of a beach
ball.

00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:26.930
To give you a sense of what we're dealing
so we can see a hair on her arm but we certainly

00:03:26.930 --> 00:03:27.940
can't see these particles.

00:03:27.940 --> 00:03:32.760
So part of the challenge is let's say one
of the places people have looked for titanium

00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:38.550
dioxide which is in sunscreen nanoparticles
is actually in the filters of swimming pools.

00:03:38.550 --> 00:03:43.210
So the concept is if it washes off people's
skin we can go there and look for it.

00:03:43.210 --> 00:03:47.091
The other place they'll find themselves from
consumer use where they're not you know for

00:03:47.091 --> 00:03:51.470
example bound in glass is at the end of a
wastewater treatment plant after we wash our

00:03:51.470 --> 00:03:52.470
clothes for example.

00:03:52.470 --> 00:03:57.610
And nanomaterials may come off they get washed
down the sink, they go to a treatment plant.

00:03:57.610 --> 00:03:58.690
They're not going to be treated there.

00:03:58.690 --> 00:04:03.060
They're not going to break down and they end
up in the sludge which is often used as agricultural

00:04:03.060 --> 00:04:07.680
amendments so that particular area is one
that's of great interest the interactions

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:11.000
of the materials with plants and what happens
when they get into soils.

00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:13.110
So these are not things that you're going
to smell.

00:04:13.110 --> 00:04:17.180
The one thing I can say about most nanoparticles
is they don't they, my term as a chemist they

00:04:17.180 --> 00:04:18.180
have no vapor pressure.

00:04:18.180 --> 00:04:20.419
They're not going to loft into the air.

00:04:20.419 --> 00:04:25.560
Really there are things in our food at least
the examples I'm giving we can talk about

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:31.470
aerosols but most of those applications we're
worried about eventually the sinks are going

00:04:31.470 --> 00:04:35.920
to be sediments and soils at least from a
big environmental perspective.

00:04:35.920 --> 00:04:40.590
We'll touch on some aspects of this again
in a few minutes but when I'm looking at that

00:04:40.590 --> 00:04:45.650
dryer fluff, like what kinds of things would
it be unremarkable to see there?

00:04:45.650 --> 00:04:47.420
Well that's a really interesting question.

00:04:47.420 --> 00:04:48.760
Everybody knows their dryer fluff, right.

00:04:48.760 --> 00:04:51.820
So if you could zoom in to this dryer fluff.

00:04:51.820 --> 00:04:56.280
So a lot of clothes now are manufactured with
silver nanoparticles.

00:04:56.280 --> 00:05:01.740
My grad students like to joke about it because
it's if you have clothes that have silver

00:05:01.740 --> 00:05:05.560
nanoparticles embedded they will be anti-microbial
so you can in principle wear your underwear

00:05:05.560 --> 00:05:06.560
for a month.

00:05:06.560 --> 00:05:08.110
They seem to like it, graduate students.

00:05:08.110 --> 00:05:10.480
My son will be very happy to hear that.

00:05:10.480 --> 00:05:16.590
So if we value that as a society some of us
may then those particles are attached but

00:05:16.590 --> 00:05:21.470
they're not they're not like embedded in glass
and they can fall off and when they fall off

00:05:21.470 --> 00:05:25.420
they can end up in dryer lint and you can
pick it up and that particle may end up on

00:05:25.420 --> 00:05:30.310
your hand it may end up part of your clothing
and there's a variety of ways in which you

00:05:30.310 --> 00:05:34.610
could expose yourself to it or you throw it
in your garbage and it ends up in the waste

00:05:34.610 --> 00:05:35.950
dump of your local community.

00:05:35.950 --> 00:05:41.310
So the point is there's not a lot of mechanisms
for nanoparticles to be degraded and once

00:05:41.310 --> 00:05:46.110
they're in water or soil they tend to stay
there unless there's some other process that

00:05:46.110 --> 00:05:47.110
occurs.

00:05:47.110 --> 00:05:51.160
Yeah, so now we'll move to the toxicity question
we know it's flowing in around it’s in the

00:05:51.160 --> 00:05:52.570
dryer fluff.

00:05:52.570 --> 00:05:58.830
But Som, Som is a preferred sort of shorthand
first name for you, Som?

00:05:58.830 --> 00:06:00.780
S-O-M, not something else.

00:06:00.780 --> 00:06:01.780
Oh yeah okay.

00:06:01.780 --> 00:06:07.950
I see you said your students sometimes call
you something else, but we won’t talk about

00:06:07.950 --> 00:06:08.950
that.

00:06:08.950 --> 00:06:11.880
So toxicity, how do you go about, how did
you become interested in this question?

00:06:11.880 --> 00:06:16.700
Well the whole nano, I got interested when
I was a child actually.

00:06:16.700 --> 00:06:20.400
You have something called a plant, touch-me-not
plant.

00:06:20.400 --> 00:06:22.690
And you touch it.

00:06:22.690 --> 00:06:25.460
The leaves will fold and the stem will drop.

00:06:25.460 --> 00:06:27.900
That's because there’s a nano structure.

00:06:27.900 --> 00:06:34.960
There are nano cells here and the water squeezed
from it and it is brought down.

00:06:34.960 --> 00:06:37.220
And that's where I got interested first.

00:06:37.220 --> 00:06:38.419
And of course I didn't know why.

00:06:38.419 --> 00:06:43.650
And also some of the some of the animals like
chameleon can change its color because of

00:06:43.650 --> 00:06:48.590
the different nano cells on the top and the
middle and the pigments get mixed.

00:06:48.590 --> 00:06:55.130
And so in nature there is always nano at play
and it has not been harmful.

00:06:55.130 --> 00:06:59.280
And only maybe the engineered nanoparticles
are harmful.

00:06:59.280 --> 00:07:01.730
Or they’re harmful, we’re immune to it.

00:07:01.730 --> 00:07:04.710
We got used to it, you know.

00:07:04.710 --> 00:07:09.550
The nano is not new, nano has been there for
centuries.

00:07:09.550 --> 00:07:12.900
Like you've mentioned, even before that, nano
has been there.

00:07:12.900 --> 00:07:18.810
So the question is engineered nanoparticles,
how do you mitigate that toxicity?

00:07:18.810 --> 00:07:20.530
You asked what is the opportunity.

00:07:20.530 --> 00:07:27.150
I think that's where the opportunity is, making
and following, there's a lot of work done

00:07:27.150 --> 00:07:33.889
already, how to mitigate the nano toxicity,
and then how to use it even for, using drugs

00:07:33.889 --> 00:07:38.580
in brain, to cross blood brain barrier.

00:07:38.580 --> 00:07:42.490
That's where the opportunity is, you have
all those nanostructures in brain and how

00:07:42.490 --> 00:07:44.550
can you influence it?

00:07:44.550 --> 00:07:51.370
But I also I'm interested not only in nano
in size, most of the opportunity in my mind

00:07:51.370 --> 00:07:53.290
is nano in time.

00:07:53.290 --> 00:07:57.440
You go to the millisecond, femtosecond, things
happen.

00:07:57.440 --> 00:08:03.770
When an atom comes towards each other in in
less than a femtosecond they react, and we

00:08:03.770 --> 00:08:06.180
know very little about it.

00:08:06.180 --> 00:08:11.050
One Nobel laureate who got Nobel prize following
that, he died recently.

00:08:11.050 --> 00:08:15.949
I think that's where the greatest opportunity
is because things happen so fast that that's

00:08:15.949 --> 00:08:20.620
nano in science, nano in space, not in nano
space, nano in time.

00:08:20.620 --> 00:08:21.620
Temporal.

00:08:21.620 --> 00:08:26.360
By the way is that part of the difference
between a substance at a larger scale and

00:08:26.360 --> 00:08:31.900
a substance at the atomic or molecular scale
that the reactions can be faster the way it

00:08:31.900 --> 00:08:35.469
reacts with other materials or whatever?

00:08:35.469 --> 00:08:39.099
Well there’s more surface area.

00:08:39.099 --> 00:08:44.300
And so you can take advantage of access to
the, to the atoms you're trying to use that

00:08:44.300 --> 00:08:45.300
way.

00:08:45.300 --> 00:08:49.329
That's why in catalysis we try and disperse
the material we're using and often those are

00:08:49.329 --> 00:08:55.550
expensive and so a more efficient way to use
them is to make them little, so more the atoms

00:08:55.550 --> 00:08:56.550
are exposed.

00:08:56.550 --> 00:09:02.209
So the smaller…dicing something up gives
you more surface area for the same volume

00:09:02.209 --> 00:09:03.269
and more reactivity?

00:09:03.269 --> 00:09:07.019
At some point it's all surface but also the
properties change as you get smaller and you

00:09:07.019 --> 00:09:08.759
can use that to advantage as well.

00:09:08.759 --> 00:09:11.220
So I guess the atom is the smallest most surface

00:09:11.220 --> 00:09:15.300
But you have to hold it usually in some way.

00:09:15.300 --> 00:09:17.959
So we usually support it on something else.

00:09:17.959 --> 00:09:23.740
So where would you look for toxicity questions,
you know I think we all start to think about

00:09:23.740 --> 00:09:27.629
ourselves you know as a consumer first, but
what about occupational exposure?

00:09:27.629 --> 00:09:32.970
I assume is anyone studying the sweater factories
in China?

00:09:32.970 --> 00:09:37.459
For, I would assume the first place you would
want to look to see if there is a human impact

00:09:37.459 --> 00:09:41.439
is not in in our households but in the manufacturing
center.

00:09:41.439 --> 00:09:42.920
Have you looked at that at all?

00:09:42.920 --> 00:09:48.460
Exactly the waste material, electronic waste
material that is sent to China and they break

00:09:48.460 --> 00:09:54.220
up and without any kind of protection from
the mouth they break and the toxic materials

00:09:54.220 --> 00:09:58.139
cartridges and everything is breathed all
the time.

00:09:58.139 --> 00:10:01.589
That's where some regulations are not there.

00:10:01.589 --> 00:10:07.480
Regulations are not here either in the manufacturing
and transport of material, not as much regulations

00:10:07.480 --> 00:10:08.480
there.

00:10:08.480 --> 00:10:13.259
But there, a lot of people are getting affected
and nobody's following it but they have to

00:10:13.259 --> 00:10:18.220
make a living and then the residual material
is dumped in the nearby river.

00:10:18.220 --> 00:10:19.220
So that's China and India.

00:10:19.220 --> 00:10:24.439
I would say the first place you look is in
the laboratory not in the manufacturing process,

00:10:24.439 --> 00:10:25.439
right?

00:10:25.439 --> 00:10:33.589
We have experiments we can do that will associate
classes of materials with risk or safety and

00:10:33.589 --> 00:10:39.670
so we can design the materials that we use
in manufacturing and those processes to keep

00:10:39.670 --> 00:10:43.740
the people working there and all of us, later,
safe.

00:10:43.740 --> 00:10:45.939
Wait, are you talking, in the laboratory you
mean…

00:10:45.939 --> 00:10:46.939
Doing the experiments.

00:10:46.939 --> 00:10:50.379
…in terms of knowing that there is a pathway
that could produce a pathology?

00:10:50.379 --> 00:10:51.379
That's right.

00:10:51.379 --> 00:10:56.889
So choosing the materials with safety in mind
and choosing the manufacturing processes with

00:10:56.889 --> 00:10:57.889
safety in mind.

00:10:57.889 --> 00:11:02.470
So there are, that's a that's a big effort
Vicki led one of those before she moved to

00:11:02.470 --> 00:11:05.059
Brown when she was back at Rice University.

00:11:05.059 --> 00:11:10.149
Andre now at our university and a number of
other places around the world study exactly

00:11:10.149 --> 00:11:11.149
that.

00:11:11.149 --> 00:11:17.249
Are there classes of materials that we can
proceed safely to commercialization and are

00:11:17.249 --> 00:11:22.950
there others where we need to understand more
about either the safety issues or define the

00:11:22.950 --> 00:11:27.610
materials themselves better to understand
which would be safe to use and which not.

00:11:27.610 --> 00:11:34.369
And really we can guide technology development
by the results of you know what we learn.

00:11:34.369 --> 00:11:40.839
The, the, the exciting part of nano is it’s
at the scale of function of biology.

00:11:40.839 --> 00:11:44.660
Som mentioned that already, I’m married
to a neuroscientist who likes to say, well

00:11:44.660 --> 00:11:49.100
the brain's always been nano where have you
people been for millions of years?

00:11:49.100 --> 00:11:55.110
And, you know, when you study the biological
interactions, some are bad and some are good.

00:11:55.110 --> 00:12:01.139
And so there are tremendous potentials for
therapy, therapies developed with those biological

00:12:01.139 --> 00:12:06.439
interactions and part of the fun really is
going in and seeing OK well not only are these

00:12:06.439 --> 00:12:10.089
materials safe, but they're protective in
some ways.

00:12:10.089 --> 00:12:13.360
So Vicki, can you talk a little bit about
the work that was just described there you

00:12:13.360 --> 00:12:14.360
know?

00:12:14.360 --> 00:12:17.860
So I think the concept of ‘safety by design’,
so if we go back, you know I think I got into

00:12:17.860 --> 00:12:23.679
this concept because I was reading in the
ACS, American Chemical Society, our publication

00:12:23.679 --> 00:12:28.920
CNE News had just done a study of like, in
the 1990s why people don't become chemists.

00:12:28.920 --> 00:12:33.790
And it turns out it's the least popular science
especially for parents, they don't want their

00:12:33.790 --> 00:12:34.949
kids to become chemists.

00:12:34.949 --> 00:12:38.559
And one of the reasons is the association
with pollution and I'm like well “that's

00:12:38.559 --> 00:12:39.559
terrible.”

00:12:39.559 --> 00:12:40.559
Why is that?

00:12:40.559 --> 00:12:44.029
And and you sort of go back in history and
you can see chemists solved you know really

00:12:44.029 --> 00:12:46.809
tough problems by developing new kinds of
chemistry.

00:12:46.809 --> 00:12:50.279
But then there are always these environmental
gotchas right?

00:12:50.279 --> 00:12:53.690
You know ten or twenty years later and so
it had been associated with that.

00:12:53.690 --> 00:12:58.300
So the concept I think that is a lot of chemists,
green chemistry, safety by design, is maybe

00:12:58.300 --> 00:13:02.240
chemists, if we start asking ourselves really
early when we build anything novel whether

00:13:02.240 --> 00:13:04.649
it's a nanomaterial or a molecule.

00:13:04.649 --> 00:13:07.610
Hey I know this is going to do something really
good.

00:13:07.610 --> 00:13:12.329
Like for example be a great insecticide and
wipe out malaria, but on the other hand how

00:13:12.329 --> 00:13:15.170
might I also think about ways it could be
bad?

00:13:15.170 --> 00:13:20.319
And then could I get the good part of it,
the functionality I want by also minimizing

00:13:20.319 --> 00:13:21.730
what I know could be dangerous.

00:13:21.730 --> 00:13:25.529
That means we need to know the science well
enough to know what makes something dangerous.

00:13:25.529 --> 00:13:29.490
So among all of our nanomaterials you know
which ones are going to be sociopaths, so

00:13:29.490 --> 00:13:32.249
to speak, and how do we train them not to
be?

00:13:32.249 --> 00:13:34.790
If you want to use that analogy.

00:13:34.790 --> 00:13:40.249
And I think that concept that a chemist as
a maker, because that's what chemists do,

00:13:40.249 --> 00:13:46.899
has a responsibility to think about what might
be the consequences of a novel material or

00:13:46.899 --> 00:13:51.249
molecule and also what might be the benefits,
so we do both together.

00:13:51.249 --> 00:13:55.959
And it's an interesting sort of balancing
act because the two disciplines of toxicology

00:13:55.959 --> 00:13:59.230
and the applications areas are quite distinct
although fundamentally very related.

00:13:59.230 --> 00:14:06.029
But I think that's the concept, so I can give
an example it's actually in our sunscreens.

00:14:06.029 --> 00:14:10.350
So the material, this gets back to the surface
titanium dioxide absorbs ultraviolet light

00:14:10.350 --> 00:14:13.509
which is what protects our skin from radiation
of the sun.

00:14:13.509 --> 00:14:15.899
So that material is nano scale.

00:14:15.899 --> 00:14:18.670
And the reason that's a good thing in your
sunscreen is it's not white.

00:14:18.670 --> 00:14:22.100
Who is old enough here, I can't see the audience
but remember where we used to put the white

00:14:22.100 --> 00:14:23.449
on our nose.

00:14:23.449 --> 00:14:27.009
That's because titanium dioxide particles
were huge they were like big boulders and

00:14:27.009 --> 00:14:30.190
they scattered light and they kind of felt
chalky, right?

00:14:30.190 --> 00:14:36.850
So consumers love it when you have clear nice
smooth sunscreen and that's the nano scale.

00:14:36.850 --> 00:14:37.850
But it's still observing UV.

00:14:37.850 --> 00:14:41.920
The problem is it's now small enough that
it can interact with your body in ways that

00:14:41.920 --> 00:14:43.089
big boulder couldn't.

00:14:43.089 --> 00:14:47.999
And in particular the other thing about nano
scale TO2s is highly reactive and under UV

00:14:47.999 --> 00:14:52.489
light can form a, nasty free radicals which
was the point of putting it there in the first

00:14:52.489 --> 00:14:53.489
place.

00:14:53.489 --> 00:14:57.220
So in any case the safety by design solution
is to coat it it's like a peanut M&amp;M, you

00:14:57.220 --> 00:15:00.989
get the titanium dioxide in the middle but
you don't want that reactive surface.

00:15:00.989 --> 00:15:06.300
So you put on the surface an aluminum oxide
coating which doesn't affect the UV absorption,

00:15:06.300 --> 00:15:08.929
but it precludes the chemistry from happening
at the surface.

00:15:08.929 --> 00:15:13.329
So that's I think and in fact many but not
all of the manufacturers of our sunscreens

00:15:13.329 --> 00:15:17.350
have to have gone to that route to sort of
get the benefit but then going that extra

00:15:17.350 --> 00:15:22.050
step and some extra cost to make it a bi-layer
material or two component.

00:15:22.050 --> 00:15:27.259
Paul I want to go back to you a bit on, just
to make sure we have a clear picture of the

00:15:27.259 --> 00:15:29.259
landscape.

00:15:29.259 --> 00:15:37.069
Which, in the magazine and the journal that
you edit, when you look across which sectors

00:15:37.069 --> 00:15:40.139
of business are most in this arena.

00:15:40.139 --> 00:15:46.500
How much funding is coming in how much of
it is oil, renewables, clothing.

00:15:46.500 --> 00:15:49.300
Can you give us just remind us again of the
landscape of this?

00:15:49.300 --> 00:15:50.300
Sure.

00:15:50.300 --> 00:15:55.980
So we had a whole panel last night on energy
ranging from capture and harvesting and conversion

00:15:55.980 --> 00:16:01.069
to storage transmission and so forth, those
are enormous areas.

00:16:01.069 --> 00:16:03.939
Electronics has really been a driver

00:16:03.939 --> 00:16:04.949
Drugs.

00:16:04.949 --> 00:16:06.990
Drugs to a smaller, there's a lot of research.

00:16:06.990 --> 00:16:13.509
There are only a few drugs that are in in
the clinic now, there are more in trials.

00:16:13.509 --> 00:16:19.779
It would seem to be an arena where there will
be a lot of potential because a much smaller

00:16:19.779 --> 00:16:20.779
amount of something you know…

00:16:20.779 --> 00:16:25.949
Not just much smaller amount but you can you
you can put combination drugs and timed release

00:16:25.949 --> 00:16:26.949
together.

00:16:26.949 --> 00:16:30.180
Part of the problem is how do you get those
approved?

00:16:30.180 --> 00:16:37.509
So there's just working backwards trying to
make the simplest nano delivery system he

00:16:37.509 --> 00:16:38.899
could possibly make.

00:16:38.899 --> 00:16:46.769
Mark Davis at CalTech put together nano delivery
material that only required 61 volumes reporting

00:16:46.769 --> 00:16:49.829
to get approval to go to trials.

00:16:49.829 --> 00:16:51.139
Oh my god.

00:16:51.139 --> 00:16:57.089
And when people make you know much fancier
combinations, given the way things are now

00:16:57.089 --> 00:17:00.709
it's almost unimaginable that one could take
those all the way to the clinic so…

00:17:00.709 --> 00:17:04.730
That reminds me of something I read in preparing
for this which was that just looking at gold

00:17:04.730 --> 00:17:10.809
that there is like, someone did an analysis
that showed that the responsiveness or issues

00:17:10.809 --> 00:17:17.000
around gold or what was it called…at a certain
level like a mid-level there's more impact.

00:17:17.000 --> 00:17:18.420
And then less and then…

00:17:18.420 --> 00:17:22.630
Yeah it’s like Goldilocks, you know too
small is good, too big you know, it's like…

00:17:22.630 --> 00:17:24.209
Which would make a drug issue…

00:17:24.209 --> 00:17:30.210
Things that make it a good drug can also get
people queasy about potential risks and I

00:17:30.210 --> 00:17:34.320
think I think the FDA examples are good ones
because there you have a regulatory process

00:17:34.320 --> 00:17:40.870
that's very…well I won't say adept but is
set up to handle novelty because it's constantly

00:17:40.870 --> 00:17:46.450
evaluating new things and is constantly weighing
risks versus benefits in a particular way

00:17:46.450 --> 00:17:51.799
and it has yeah it's been incredibly hard
especially for the more exciting nano materials

00:17:51.799 --> 00:17:57.299
to get through those hoops and unless the
disease it's curing is you know has no other

00:17:57.299 --> 00:17:58.560
people have no other hope.

00:17:58.560 --> 00:18:03.409
So there are certain cancers in which there's
been more progress that sort of chronic disease

00:18:03.409 --> 00:18:07.919
but I think that it's an interesting case
study because when we looked at examples from

00:18:07.919 --> 00:18:13.070
our center at Rice of things that were moving
into human trials it was on the order of about

00:18:13.070 --> 00:18:18.350
12 years from when those first papers saying
hey this could be a possibility to when it

00:18:18.350 --> 00:18:23.090
even got into human trials which is much slower
than you know probably about a factor two

00:18:23.090 --> 00:18:24.770
slower than other materials.

00:18:24.770 --> 00:18:29.799
Yeah regulation is really set up to work on
chemicals which can be precisely defined in

00:18:29.799 --> 00:18:30.909
general.

00:18:30.909 --> 00:18:38.050
And when we make new nanomaterials there are
usually mixtures and often when we manufacture

00:18:38.050 --> 00:18:43.590
them it's going to be easier to make a mixture
than to make an absolutely atomically precise

00:18:43.590 --> 00:18:44.590
structure.

00:18:44.590 --> 00:18:49.950
And so if you had to treat every new nanomaterial
like a chemical, we don't have enough money

00:18:49.950 --> 00:18:53.620
and we don't have enough animals in the world
to do the required testing and so we have

00:18:53.620 --> 00:18:55.570
to become smarter about that.

00:18:55.570 --> 00:19:01.580
And that's one of the places where that hierarchy
of trying to define groups of materials that

00:19:01.580 --> 00:19:07.890
appear safe and can be tested together, hopefully
can be commercialized safely.

00:19:07.890 --> 00:19:12.309
And we can do those more defined experiments
both on the material side and the testing

00:19:12.309 --> 00:19:14.200
side.

00:19:14.200 --> 00:19:18.360
As you know as needed to to develop other
materials and apply them.

00:19:18.360 --> 00:19:23.990
Can you talk a little bit about the work you've
done on nanoparticles and how they behave

00:19:23.990 --> 00:19:26.340
in plants and soils and stuff like that.

00:19:26.340 --> 00:19:28.510
Yeah I mean soils are fascinating.

00:19:28.510 --> 00:19:31.580
I may go off a little bit of tangent here.

00:19:31.580 --> 00:19:38.250
So plants are remarkable living systems because
now I am not a plant biologist I was fortunate

00:19:38.250 --> 00:19:42.529
to work with some really good ones like a
lot of nano scientists and what they do is

00:19:42.529 --> 00:19:46.860
they are really good at mobilizing what they
need from the soil and they do that in partnership

00:19:46.860 --> 00:19:49.510
with microbes that grow at their roots.

00:19:49.510 --> 00:19:53.470
And so when a nanoparticle ends up there remember
I said the reason we're studying plants is

00:19:53.470 --> 00:19:57.500
we're pretty sure that's going to be a sink
for a lot of nanomaterials coming out of wastewater

00:19:57.500 --> 00:19:58.500
treatment.

00:19:58.500 --> 00:20:01.600
So consumer applications and others, that's
where they'll be.

00:20:01.600 --> 00:20:07.210
And so what plants can do is you sort of have
this image that there is an organism and there's

00:20:07.210 --> 00:20:10.990
a nanoparticle and they're going to interact
and the nanoparticle is going to kind of stay

00:20:10.990 --> 00:20:11.990
the nanoparticle.

00:20:11.990 --> 00:20:16.070
But actually the chemistry that happens in
the roots not even in the plant but at the

00:20:16.070 --> 00:20:21.180
root system can be very aggressive and it
can actually dissolve and break down nanomaterials

00:20:21.180 --> 00:20:25.110
quite quickly in a way that we don't see in
other organisms.

00:20:25.110 --> 00:20:29.809
And so one of the features of a lot of the
emerging nano plant studies is that if you've

00:20:29.809 --> 00:20:33.389
got nano materials in soils and they're really
close to root systems, they’re going to

00:20:33.389 --> 00:20:34.389
dissolve.

00:20:34.389 --> 00:20:38.309
They're going to become soluble and the plant
is going to try to extract elements from that

00:20:38.309 --> 00:20:42.610
and that can be a good and bad thing depending
on the plant system.

00:20:42.610 --> 00:20:47.320
The other thing we learned is that most plants
don't take up whole nanoparticles.

00:20:47.320 --> 00:20:48.320
Plants are very tight.

00:20:48.320 --> 00:20:52.490
They don't, they've developed a lot of defenses
against plant viruses which by the way one

00:20:52.490 --> 00:20:55.780
of my favorite plant virus is cowpea mosaic
virus is a 15 nanometer sphere.

00:20:55.780 --> 00:21:00.940
So the only way it can get into a plant is
with an insect punching a leaf.

00:21:00.940 --> 00:21:07.250
So plants have actually, they’re quite difficult
to get into and their modulation that the

00:21:07.250 --> 00:21:11.960
plants activity at the root system seems to
be the dominant issue and also plants draw

00:21:11.960 --> 00:21:14.370
enormous amounts of water from soil.

00:21:14.370 --> 00:21:20.299
So my colleague was doing work with cottonwoods
and they would just draw all the nanoparticles

00:21:20.299 --> 00:21:25.110
as huge pressure so you could have them everywhere
in the soil but they get pulled just because

00:21:25.110 --> 00:21:27.190
of the water flow into the plant root system.

00:21:27.190 --> 00:21:32.860
So the exposures accumulate at the roots where
they undergo—now the one really not such

00:21:32.860 --> 00:21:37.880
a good thing that I think can happen is some
nanomaterials or anti-microbial, and the microbial

00:21:37.880 --> 00:21:41.640
communities in plant root systems are crucial
for doing what they do.

00:21:41.640 --> 00:21:45.289
And so when that gets knocked out the plants
get extremely unhealthy.

00:21:45.289 --> 00:21:49.509
And that's really the primary way that you
can affect agricultural productivity but you

00:21:49.509 --> 00:21:53.960
can also see with some other materials you
can get you know actually plants grow better.

00:21:53.960 --> 00:21:58.429
We had one experiment we did where we saw
an enhancement of our plant growth and that

00:21:58.429 --> 00:22:02.320
was partly because of the minerals that were
getting released by the nanoparticles.

00:22:02.320 --> 00:22:03.970
Is there a prospect…

00:22:03.970 --> 00:22:07.430
One important use of nanoparticles in agriculture.

00:22:07.430 --> 00:22:12.039
One of the main problems in agriculture is
nitrogen utilization efficiency.

00:22:12.039 --> 00:22:18.600
Lot of fertilizer is wasted and that nitrogen
utilization efficiency can be improved by

00:22:18.600 --> 00:22:19.600
using nanoparticles.

00:22:19.600 --> 00:22:25.460
By that I mean you dilute nanoparticles to
the root to deliver the nutrients and nitrogen

00:22:25.460 --> 00:22:26.460
there.

00:22:26.460 --> 00:22:29.030
So that on the top it's not wasted.

00:22:29.030 --> 00:22:37.289
And I think you've mentioned the enzymes that
the that the roots generate can extract things

00:22:37.289 --> 00:22:39.970
from the nanoparticles, if the nanoparticles
are properly designed.

00:22:39.970 --> 00:22:42.710
There's a lot of opportunities there.

00:22:42.710 --> 00:22:43.710
Well this is good.

00:22:43.710 --> 00:22:45.509
I wanted to shift back into the prospects.

00:22:45.509 --> 00:22:51.309
We've talked a lot right now already about
some of the questions about impacts but.

00:22:51.309 --> 00:22:56.429
You know risks and benefits tradeoffs is always
there so…is there anything else in agriculture

00:22:56.429 --> 00:23:01.370
that sounds like a pretty interesting idea
because precision delivery of anything is

00:23:01.370 --> 00:23:02.370
what you want.

00:23:02.370 --> 00:23:05.049
Another thing is to imitate we have a bio
solar project.

00:23:05.049 --> 00:23:07.520
To imitate the plants, what the plants are
doing.

00:23:07.520 --> 00:23:09.690
The leaves collect the photons and collect
electrons.

00:23:09.690 --> 00:23:15.320
That all depends again on the nanostructure
of the leaves and how do they how do you increase

00:23:15.320 --> 00:23:23.270
the efficiency of conventional photons electrons
is the problem that the efficiency is very

00:23:23.270 --> 00:23:24.270
low.

00:23:24.270 --> 00:23:27.770
People think that in general by controlling
the morphology of the enzymes the way they

00:23:27.770 --> 00:23:33.000
dance with each other I think the efficiency
can be increased.

00:23:33.000 --> 00:23:40.690
By the way I don't use in the ADF analogy
to the the I have a project called carbon

00:23:40.690 --> 00:23:45.309
dioxide to champagne, dioxide is now no problem
but carbon tax of champagne.

00:23:45.309 --> 00:23:51.070
Carbon dioxide can go into carbonate to formate
various alcohols.

00:23:51.070 --> 00:23:54.190
They all have pathways with activation barrier.

00:23:54.190 --> 00:24:00.970
If you can selectively decrease the activation
barrier for carbon dioxide to ethanol we are

00:24:00.970 --> 00:24:04.760
like ethanol right or formate is better ethanol.

00:24:04.760 --> 00:24:08.539
Selectively decrease that activation barrier
that to be very good.

00:24:08.539 --> 00:24:13.279
And we can do that using copper nanoparticles
that are doped.

00:24:13.279 --> 00:24:14.750
Why copper we don't know.

00:24:14.750 --> 00:24:16.779
But doped that again shows the heterogeneity.

00:24:16.779 --> 00:24:22.509
Their doped means only a few atoms of water
you dope with in the copper does increase

00:24:22.509 --> 00:24:25.929
the activation barrier as carbon dioxide goes
to formate and then alcohol.

00:24:25.929 --> 00:24:30.690
So that's a good example of the beneficial
use of the heterogeneity but I have to know

00:24:30.690 --> 00:24:31.690
that.

00:24:31.690 --> 00:24:35.240
So I wanted to talk to you more about medicine
we talked about medicines pharmaceuticals

00:24:35.240 --> 00:24:39.179
but where else in medicine can this play a
role?

00:24:39.179 --> 00:24:41.129
In health and health care generally?

00:24:41.129 --> 00:24:48.260
Yeah so I mean just in sunscreen is a simple
example we can build in capabilities to nanoparticles

00:24:48.260 --> 00:24:49.920
that we apply there.

00:24:49.920 --> 00:24:56.740
There are tremendous opportunities in diagnostics
so we can we don't have sensors that we put

00:24:56.740 --> 00:25:00.539
on as—they’re called temporary tattoos,
but they’re really stickers.

00:25:00.539 --> 00:25:04.730
There's one that’s just being distributed
now to look at your UV exposure I think L’Oreal’s

00:25:04.730 --> 00:25:07.970
giving it out in a dozen or so countries to
say…

00:25:07.970 --> 00:25:10.379
So it's a cumulative exposure thing?

00:25:10.379 --> 00:25:18.639
Right and so, these you know a lot of people
have Fitbits, I think Vicki has one on or

00:25:18.639 --> 00:25:21.020
some equivalent right that measure exercise.

00:25:21.020 --> 00:25:23.593
There's a lot more that we could measure.

00:25:23.593 --> 00:25:24.889
You can tell when someone's dehydrated.

00:25:24.889 --> 00:25:32.039
For athletes there’re sweat sensors they
can see that you know the composition lines

00:25:32.039 --> 00:25:34.970
coming out of you are changing over time and
that can be an alert.

00:25:34.970 --> 00:25:42.220
You know you need to you need to stop at the
next table in the marathon to pick up more

00:25:42.220 --> 00:25:43.710
water electrolytes there.

00:25:43.710 --> 00:25:50.010
The great opportunity I think is in medicine
is delivering where the cancer cells are.

00:25:50.010 --> 00:25:56.080
I use the example of when you take aspirin
you have no side effect but if you have chemotherapy

00:25:56.080 --> 00:25:57.799
lose hair and everything.

00:25:57.799 --> 00:26:04.110
But if you can use that that chemical it can
kill the cell only where the tumor cell is,

00:26:04.110 --> 00:26:09.679
then chemotherapy will be just like aspirin
and it is possible now, people like at MIT

00:26:09.679 --> 00:26:15.980
has shown that using nanoparticles, it can
go there and attack where the tumor cell is

00:26:15.980 --> 00:26:16.980
and no other cell.

00:26:16.980 --> 00:26:21.279
And that will be the really the holy grail
I think.

00:26:21.279 --> 00:26:24.009
Attack where the damage is.

00:26:24.009 --> 00:26:25.620
I would think a little bigger.

00:26:25.620 --> 00:26:30.580
I mean first of all I'm not sure we can make
a heat seeking missile yet to a cancer…

00:26:30.580 --> 00:26:34.809
…but they can, they can…missile drugs.

00:26:34.809 --> 00:26:35.809
Lot of people do that…

00:26:35.809 --> 00:26:37.419
Yeah but their efficiency is pretty low.

00:26:37.419 --> 00:26:39.549
Meaning if you take a dose, it’s better…

00:26:39.549 --> 00:26:41.820
I know people who are alive because of his
technology…

00:26:41.820 --> 00:26:43.940
No I'm not saying it's not.

00:26:43.940 --> 00:26:48.519
I'm just saying we should be cautious in saying
that 100 percent of the drug goes to the cancer

00:26:48.519 --> 00:26:52.379
cell because it's rare that that happens.

00:26:52.379 --> 00:26:56.350
But it is true side effects can be reduced
by delivering properly…

00:26:56.350 --> 00:27:00.379
But I think really you know kind of one of
the other themes at this festival that I think

00:27:00.379 --> 00:27:05.240
is really an interesting question is if you're
going to make materials artificial materials

00:27:05.240 --> 00:27:08.220
that integrate with our bodies and that's
really what we're starting to do with these

00:27:08.220 --> 00:27:12.789
devices like actually become part of it, what
is that interface going to look like and what

00:27:12.789 --> 00:27:17.240
we've been doing to date is taking large boulders
you know macroscopic materials and shoving

00:27:17.240 --> 00:27:18.879
them into people's bodies.

00:27:18.879 --> 00:27:23.409
And of course the body is not happy with that
it's got a lot of reactions to having a macroscopic

00:27:23.409 --> 00:27:25.460
element put inside of it.

00:27:25.460 --> 00:27:30.370
So you know my hypothesis is that actually
nano structured materials are the ideal thing

00:27:30.370 --> 00:27:35.309
to help us make kind of this seamless integration
between the artificial world and the living

00:27:35.309 --> 00:27:40.580
systems now doing that has a lot of consequences
and presents a lot of challenges.

00:27:40.580 --> 00:27:44.929
But I believe that that's what you know if
you were to go 50 years in the future you're

00:27:44.929 --> 00:27:51.710
going to find the tattoos or you know implants
in the brain or you know our clothing, the

00:27:51.710 --> 00:27:55.690
ways in which it will interact with this will
be largely defined by materials and with nanoscale

00:27:55.690 --> 00:27:57.879
structures and dimensions.

00:27:57.879 --> 00:28:02.440
What's for this forward on that front already,
is there some particular niche?

00:28:02.440 --> 00:28:06.690
Well there’s…I’m looking at Paul seeing
if he wants to pick one of the sci fi examples

00:28:06.690 --> 00:28:08.009
or if I should.

00:28:08.009 --> 00:28:15.559
Well you know there is plenty of accessible
space on us so for instance there are two

00:28:15.559 --> 00:28:18.350
or three hundred species that live in your
mouth.

00:28:18.350 --> 00:28:19.350
Some are protective.

00:28:19.350 --> 00:28:25.140
Some turn on you and give you cavities or
root cavities or if you are going to get a

00:28:25.140 --> 00:28:29.799
periodontal implant they're going to eat around
the implant it's going to fall out.

00:28:29.799 --> 00:28:35.279
And dentists and periodontists know that but
they don't yet have any reconnaissance for

00:28:35.279 --> 00:28:37.559
those species.

00:28:37.559 --> 00:28:44.210
It isn't that difficult to imagine when you're
getting ready for an implant to get a swipe

00:28:44.210 --> 00:28:48.450
and check if you have that bug for the month
preceding.

00:28:48.450 --> 00:28:50.649
And so those kinds of things will come.

00:28:50.649 --> 00:28:54.620
I think much sooner than the ten or twenty
years I'm hoping you know in the next few

00:28:54.620 --> 00:28:59.330
months we develop the technology and then
it gets out there very quickly.

00:28:59.330 --> 00:29:04.690
I'll give you the other example and you'll
have to tell me how sci-fi this is.

00:29:04.690 --> 00:29:09.262
But for example we were just talking about
this before we came on the number of people

00:29:09.262 --> 00:29:14.279
now and the opportunities in neuroscience
and neurology for the implantation directly

00:29:14.279 --> 00:29:15.940
of electrodes into people's brains for treating
disease.

00:29:15.940 --> 00:29:21.320
And the problem with that is many many many
problems.

00:29:21.320 --> 00:29:25.549
But if you could make those electrodes highly
conductive and also flexible that's something

00:29:25.549 --> 00:29:29.700
that biological systems are very mechanically
sensitive.

00:29:29.700 --> 00:29:35.179
Almost all examples I've seen would tell us
that and so if you can integrate those electrodes

00:29:35.179 --> 00:29:39.880
and make them less foreign to our brains then
what you're going to find is they last longer

00:29:39.880 --> 00:29:44.669
they have less side effects and that's something
that you can do by manipulating material structure

00:29:44.669 --> 00:29:46.129
on the nanoscale.

00:29:46.129 --> 00:29:48.740
I mean that's a far reach, so I'll look at
Paul…

00:29:48.740 --> 00:29:52.870
Vicki’s very kind because it's something
my wife and I work on together.

00:29:52.870 --> 00:29:57.519
That reminds me I’ll just ask this out of
the blue…when I did my master's thesis in

00:29:57.519 --> 00:30:01.879
journalism at Columbia I was on organ transplantation,
the future of organ transplantation.

00:30:01.879 --> 00:30:07.789
I did a piece about a profile later for Discover
Magazine of a organ transplant recovery coordinator

00:30:07.789 --> 00:30:11.419
and lately I've been tracking literature.

00:30:11.419 --> 00:30:16.690
Because as we move to autonomous vehicles
and stuff, the number of donors is going to

00:30:16.690 --> 00:30:20.749
become a…it's already a problem it's going
to be a huge problem so is there any role

00:30:20.749 --> 00:30:23.549
here for antirejection with organs?

00:30:23.549 --> 00:30:27.179
Yeah several yeah there's several things in
transplants or hospitals sort of built on

00:30:27.179 --> 00:30:32.429
the back of some fantastic transplant surgeons
and you know we have a problem right now in

00:30:32.429 --> 00:30:37.690
terms of harvesting organs that you know someone's
in a motorcycle accident and they get caught

00:30:37.690 --> 00:30:44.230
up and you know ship to a bunch of different
patients and Singapore last year six people

00:30:44.230 --> 00:30:49.000
died from one donor from infected organs and
that's something that happens regularly, there’s

00:30:49.000 --> 00:30:56.299
not time for reconnaissance you know from
the point of collection of the donor to implantation.

00:30:56.299 --> 00:31:04.399
And you know as you know if you follow transplantation
all kinds of things happened with immunosuppression

00:31:04.399 --> 00:31:10.169
we basically give an artificial cancer to
many of the recipients that leaves them in

00:31:10.169 --> 00:31:16.259
the hospital for months afterwards under protection.

00:31:16.259 --> 00:31:20.919
Because of their first compromised immune
system and then the diseases that we induce.

00:31:20.919 --> 00:31:28.340
And so there are all kinds of opportunities
there and connections being made really between

00:31:28.340 --> 00:31:35.580
the transplant surgeons and the people in
nanomedicine to try and overcome some of those

00:31:35.580 --> 00:31:37.110
some of these issues.

00:31:37.110 --> 00:31:45.470
Interesting one thing we were talking about
earlier backstage was how your specializations

00:31:45.470 --> 00:31:50.460
can have potential value in a host of different
disciplines and you tend to float I think

00:31:50.460 --> 00:31:56.330
when you’re talking about floating it seems
that collaborative approaches to the science

00:31:56.330 --> 00:31:58.770
and innovation seem to be vital.

00:31:58.770 --> 00:32:03.929
I thought maybe I’d be curious for each
of you to describe whether you've had an experience

00:32:03.929 --> 00:32:06.789
where that's become manifest.

00:32:06.789 --> 00:32:07.789
Vicki.

00:32:07.789 --> 00:32:14.119
I yeah I mean I think if you're a nano scientist
you actually are a collaborator.

00:32:14.119 --> 00:32:18.143
The field was sort of started in that way
in many ways.

00:32:18.143 --> 00:32:22.980
So what my interesting observation about collaboration
is is I sort of say two things to my students

00:32:22.980 --> 00:32:28.519
because virtually everything I do is collaborative
you know first of all different disciplines

00:32:28.519 --> 00:32:30.669
present different challenges for collaboration.

00:32:30.669 --> 00:32:36.070
So I've learned through being a nano scientist
that dealing with toxicologists they have

00:32:36.070 --> 00:32:40.200
a culture and they have a thing that they…so
there's a value system that comes with your

00:32:40.200 --> 00:32:43.950
your core discipline versus dealing with physicists
they’re going to have completely different

00:32:43.950 --> 00:32:45.090
sets of issues.

00:32:45.090 --> 00:32:49.520
So part of working well and understanding
these disciplines is to know what are they

00:32:49.520 --> 00:32:54.499
going to really care about and how do you…so
it isn't just communication and terminology

00:32:54.499 --> 00:32:59.740
but it's understanding what am I going to
value my core training is as a chemist versus

00:32:59.740 --> 00:33:03.049
what they're going to value and there's something
about I think it's shaped when people are

00:33:03.049 --> 00:33:08.820
undergraduates this sort of disciplinary identity
and reinforced in graduate school so part

00:33:08.820 --> 00:33:14.559
of the part of the gulf is to share of what's
going to be important when you do science.

00:33:14.559 --> 00:33:17.150
How many replicate experiments are you going
to have to do.

00:33:17.150 --> 00:33:22.249
And of course there's the language figuring
out you know my favorite example in nano is

00:33:22.249 --> 00:33:26.289
if you're a chemist you learn how two atoms
interact through something called bond theory

00:33:26.289 --> 00:33:30.960
and if you're a physicist you learn solid
state structure and they seem very different

00:33:30.960 --> 00:33:32.090
but they're actually the same, right.

00:33:32.090 --> 00:33:37.269
And so the field I think revealed that as
those two areas came together.

00:33:37.269 --> 00:33:44.860
But it is really I think a matter of really
appreciating that even though science is objective

00:33:44.860 --> 00:33:49.289
disciplines have value systems and part of
effective collaboration is kind of understand

00:33:49.289 --> 00:33:54.580
oh I'm working with a surgeon they're going
to be really focused on a quick tool and they're

00:33:54.580 --> 00:33:57.090
going be very focused on patients as opposed
to dealing with a physicist…

00:33:57.090 --> 00:33:58.869
And they’re going to be very egotistical…

00:33:58.869 --> 00:34:02.080
Yeah they’re going to be very busy and they’re
going to be very arrogant and willing to try

00:34:02.080 --> 00:34:03.080
anything.

00:34:03.080 --> 00:34:04.159
In my limited experience.

00:34:04.159 --> 00:34:10.119
And I think as scientists we sometimes don't
want to talk about you know culture within

00:34:10.119 --> 00:34:14.460
science or the personality elements and I
don't know if I'm going that far but I think

00:34:14.460 --> 00:34:18.860
in collaboration it's really revealed to me
how important that is and when you get that

00:34:18.860 --> 00:34:23.610
right and you understand them and they understand
you is when you usually are able to have a

00:34:23.610 --> 00:34:27.800
really great working relationship and make
you know make the progress you need.

00:34:27.800 --> 00:34:30.170
Now we're going to move into the sort of the
interface with society more generally.

00:34:30.170 --> 00:34:32.650
A couple of things that come to mind.

00:34:32.650 --> 00:34:39.560
One is to ask you which lessons from history
worry you the most when you think about where

00:34:39.560 --> 00:34:46.120
nano is going to go now, whether it's looking
at what happened with DDT or more recently

00:34:46.120 --> 00:34:51.650
with GMOs where you have this Monsanto effect
where most people who many people who have

00:34:51.650 --> 00:34:55.730
a concern about GMOs it's really their concern
about industry or about something about a

00:34:55.730 --> 00:35:00.680
particular company and and a lot of the interest
in this arena are corporate as well.

00:35:00.680 --> 00:35:06.100
So I don't know if you each have a sense of
kind of like your worry about how this is

00:35:06.100 --> 00:35:07.500
going to play out going forward.

00:35:07.500 --> 00:35:11.330
I think the whole reason we're here is we're
trying to take a thoughtful approach.

00:35:11.330 --> 00:35:18.500
The field has established some of the leading
investigators who work at that interface of

00:35:18.500 --> 00:35:23.720
materials and safety as well as new materials
and medicine.

00:35:23.720 --> 00:35:30.800
When you do essentially the same measurement
looking for positive and negative effects

00:35:30.800 --> 00:35:35.800
and so really what you're doing is sorting
and when you combine those those areas you

00:35:35.800 --> 00:35:40.890
learn more the biology and you know you're
able to do an end run you know maybe around

00:35:40.890 --> 00:35:49.000
the complete characterization in some cases
and then identify you know where one needs

00:35:49.000 --> 00:35:50.030
to know more.

00:35:50.030 --> 00:35:56.890
And I think the next step for the field and
commercialization is making some choices as

00:35:56.890 --> 00:36:01.530
to which technologies to push based on the
results.

00:36:01.530 --> 00:36:07.020
And I think we’re right at that crossroads
now where we have so many different possibilities

00:36:07.020 --> 00:36:14.390
in so many different areas of application
that if we put that in as part of the feedback

00:36:14.390 --> 00:36:18.860
loop then that's where we avoid the missteps
of other fields in the past.

00:36:18.860 --> 00:36:24.701
that we've got what is important may be, asbestos
was very good for insulation and everything

00:36:24.701 --> 00:36:26.800
it was good for the society.

00:36:26.800 --> 00:36:32.510
And then there was asbestosis just wanted
to make sure that it's no nanosys around the

00:36:32.510 --> 00:36:33.510
corner.

00:36:33.510 --> 00:36:38.370
And if you ignore it and if we don't have
the technology to mitigate it then there'll

00:36:38.370 --> 00:36:43.630
be nanosys and there will be I suppose if
you spend more money now to remove asbestos

00:36:43.630 --> 00:36:46.540
and nanoparticles would be even harder to
catch.

00:36:46.540 --> 00:36:53.010
So that is what we have learned from asbestosis
and whatever that term is called, silicosis.

00:36:53.010 --> 00:36:55.510
They are all good, all these good materials.

00:36:55.510 --> 00:37:01.820
They have benefits like you said you have
to also know the side effects and even medicines

00:37:01.820 --> 00:37:02.820
have side effects.

00:37:02.820 --> 00:37:08.810
You know so we have to swallow that but try
to control the side effects.

00:37:08.810 --> 00:37:12.980
So this gets this gets back to the safety
by design paradigm.

00:37:12.980 --> 00:37:13.980
Can you do better.

00:37:13.980 --> 00:37:17.260
Do we always have to learn by going uh oh
we didn't think of that?

00:37:17.260 --> 00:37:19.170
Yeah I mean I think so.

00:37:19.170 --> 00:37:21.530
I think my worry is sort of twofold.

00:37:21.530 --> 00:37:25.670
First of all you know even though we're talking
about nano and its risks it's actually kind

00:37:25.670 --> 00:37:30.080
of a laboratory experiment for the first time
a discipline is really trying to get ahead

00:37:30.080 --> 00:37:33.630
of the curve since we know the technology
has unintended consequences.

00:37:33.630 --> 00:37:38.130
I mean I can't think of one maybe somebody
will come up with one that is all good and

00:37:38.130 --> 00:37:39.130
everybody loved it.

00:37:39.130 --> 00:37:40.470
Forever and ever.

00:37:40.470 --> 00:37:44.570
But my sense is that we're kind of trying
to figure out how it works…

00:37:44.570 --> 00:37:45.640
Chocolate, actually…

00:37:45.640 --> 00:37:46.640
Chocolate?

00:37:46.640 --> 00:37:47.640
I'm sure.

00:37:47.640 --> 00:37:53.660
No I get migraines from chocolate, so I’ll
fight you on that..

00:37:53.660 --> 00:37:59.020
So my sense is that you know society has to
grapple and by society I mean the public of

00:37:59.020 --> 00:38:00.530
which I am one.

00:38:00.530 --> 00:38:04.310
With…we want the benefits like we want our
Fitbits.

00:38:04.310 --> 00:38:08.250
And if you sold me a Fitbit that worked better
because it actually was part of my body I

00:38:08.250 --> 00:38:10.100
would probably buy it.

00:38:10.100 --> 00:38:14.950
But you know so the consumer demand for better
sunscreens that are not white and chalky is

00:38:14.950 --> 00:38:16.740
sort of what's driving this process.

00:38:16.740 --> 00:38:22.320
And I think that's partly an area that I wonder
how consumers are going to be part of this

00:38:22.320 --> 00:38:26.880
and saying hey I want a label or I want this
to slow down here's where I'm willing to accept

00:38:26.880 --> 00:38:29.020
a risk here's where I'm not willing to accept
the risk.

00:38:29.020 --> 00:38:33.720
And I think that there's a there's a challenge
there that I worry about which comes actually

00:38:33.720 --> 00:38:38.050
with labeling because I don't think consumers
know necessarily and I think that's keeping

00:38:38.050 --> 00:38:42.440
them out of some of these some of the ways
in which they may influence this large sort

00:38:42.440 --> 00:38:47.960
of corporate academic sort of you know train
that’s left the station so to speak.

00:38:47.960 --> 00:38:48.980
On the science front

00:38:48.980 --> 00:38:53.250
You know I'm going to and this is a broken
record.

00:38:53.250 --> 00:38:57.450
I'm going to worry about the field moving
too fast towards trying to do testing rather

00:38:57.450 --> 00:39:01.440
than trying to establish the general principles
by which we can do safety by design.

00:39:01.440 --> 00:39:05.900
So if you've got a product that's got a sunscreen
you want to know how do I know if it's toxic

00:39:05.900 --> 00:39:10.000
and that does us no good in helping us design
it more safely.

00:39:10.000 --> 00:39:14.420
We actually have to do a little bit more fundamental
science and it is extraordinarily hard in

00:39:14.420 --> 00:39:15.600
this particular climate.

00:39:15.600 --> 00:39:18.100
I didn't mean to say climate.

00:39:18.100 --> 00:39:20.290
I was going to try to avoid that word.

00:39:20.290 --> 00:39:29.280
To try to make that case for slower more methodical
work that is linked to that potential.

00:39:29.280 --> 00:39:33.000
And I think you know going too fast you won't
be able to develop these really fundamental

00:39:33.000 --> 00:39:38.050
mechanistic understandings that you really
need to do effective safety by design.

00:39:38.050 --> 00:39:39.050
So I think that's my scientific worry.

00:39:39.050 --> 00:39:45.480
But Som this gets back to something you kind
of mentioned in passing talking about sunscreens

00:39:45.480 --> 00:39:51.850
that there are natural solutions that might
be more beneficial but often if you're a company,

00:39:51.850 --> 00:39:59.880
big cosmetic manufacturer or something your
interest is efficiency and production and

00:39:59.880 --> 00:40:01.170
profitability.

00:40:01.170 --> 00:40:06.790
And and you know consumer confidence but it
might lead you in a direction that wouldn't

00:40:06.790 --> 00:40:11.990
necessarily be in the end the the best choice
for what you were talking about.

00:40:11.990 --> 00:40:18.820
I mean when all the money flowing into the
field how much of it is related to the sustainability

00:40:18.820 --> 00:40:24.890
questions or as opposed to the, boy can we
manufacture sweaters more efficiently now

00:40:24.890 --> 00:40:25.890
or whatever?

00:40:25.890 --> 00:40:32.150
A while ago, at least four years ago the consumers
were wanting more green products but then

00:40:32.150 --> 00:40:35.100
the economy became bad, they got away from
that.

00:40:35.100 --> 00:40:39.330
But there was a survey done we showed that
the consumers want green products.

00:40:39.330 --> 00:40:45.230
There was a U.S. news site I remember the
cover say go green or go out of business.

00:40:45.230 --> 00:40:48.060
There was a pitch and that was consumer group.

00:40:48.060 --> 00:40:54.860
That's because there is an association in
UK, which showed that every sample of breast

00:40:54.860 --> 00:41:01.200
milk analyzed showed cancer compound, every
not not ninety-nine point nine, every sample

00:41:01.200 --> 00:41:05.020
of the breast milk analyzed some carcinogenic
compound.

00:41:05.020 --> 00:41:12.330
So when that came out along with some other
company, which marks red blue green, then

00:41:12.330 --> 00:41:13.910
consumers are driven that way.

00:41:13.910 --> 00:41:15.830
Until the economy suffered.

00:41:15.830 --> 00:41:22.010
Let's go back to cosmetics for a second because
for me those are great thermometer of public

00:41:22.010 --> 00:41:23.010
perception.

00:41:23.010 --> 00:41:29.140
So I’ll tell you a secret, I like going
to cosmetic scanners and when you go to them

00:41:29.140 --> 00:41:35.490
in the U.S., nanos used for promotion of cosmetics
and if you go to cosmetics counters in most

00:41:35.490 --> 00:41:38.440
Asian countries you charge more for things
that are nano.

00:41:38.440 --> 00:41:42.280
And if you go in Europe you don't mention
nano.

00:41:42.280 --> 00:41:50.620
And that's something that we as a community
are trying to address so exactly the association

00:41:50.620 --> 00:41:55.400
you made that people don't think of nano as
GMO but have an understanding of what it is

00:41:55.400 --> 00:41:59.840
we're doing that isn't supplied by a science
fiction writer and it is really a race right

00:41:59.840 --> 00:42:02.410
now for that public perception.

00:42:02.410 --> 00:42:07.010
A place, let me talk about another field for
a second so plastic microspheres are being

00:42:07.010 --> 00:42:12.890
taken out of cosmetics because of the environmental
consequences and the replacement for them

00:42:12.890 --> 00:42:15.280
may well be some natural nano products.

00:42:15.280 --> 00:42:20.300
There's some that are being tested now by
a number of different companies that will

00:42:20.300 --> 00:42:28.480
be biodegradable and safe ways to deliver
the cosmetics that don't have any of the consequences

00:42:28.480 --> 00:42:29.990
that the plastics do.

00:42:29.990 --> 00:42:33.410
So I think you'll see those you'll see those.

00:42:33.410 --> 00:42:38.251
You know as soon as one can scale up the production.

00:42:38.251 --> 00:42:42.640
Opportunity in biodegradability is great but
it is controlled biodegradability that you

00:42:42.640 --> 00:42:43.640
don't know.

00:42:43.640 --> 00:42:48.290
You don't want to make it and biodegrade tomorrow,
is going to be on the shelf for six months

00:42:48.290 --> 00:42:49.290
one year.

00:42:49.290 --> 00:42:55.930
So you want it to biodegrade in maybe in one
year or something we need to get to the water

00:42:55.930 --> 00:42:56.930
and so on.

00:42:56.930 --> 00:43:01.410
So it is controlled biodegradability is the
key opportunity I think.

00:43:01.410 --> 00:43:06.060
This brings up something else which is you
mentioned Europe the difference in Europe

00:43:06.060 --> 00:43:13.200
and perception and here and in Asia in dealing
with all these issues GMOs, nuclear power.

00:43:13.200 --> 00:43:17.610
This I assume Crispr genetic technology coming
forward.

00:43:17.610 --> 00:43:22.620
I think we would like to think there's one
sense of what the precautionary principle

00:43:22.620 --> 00:43:28.810
is but I've yet to find it that everyone in
this room might have a somewhat more common

00:43:28.810 --> 00:43:32.820
sense of precaution when they look at technology
and innovation because they're sort of a select

00:43:32.820 --> 00:43:33.820
audience.

00:43:33.820 --> 00:43:39.100
But you look at the global landscape and it's
all over the place so there will be opposition.

00:43:39.100 --> 00:43:43.670
There will be people who will and organizations
that will come at you in a hard way.

00:43:43.670 --> 00:43:49.470
I've seen in the literature already that you
see headlines, one piece that was in our background

00:43:49.470 --> 00:43:55.900
briefing from Business Insider from last year
talks about…This is the Patagonia fleece

00:43:55.900 --> 00:44:00.640
finding how much of this fleece is out in
nature and this is a shocking amount of these

00:44:00.640 --> 00:44:05.260
fibers more than 64,000 pounds into oceans
and streams every day.

00:44:05.260 --> 00:44:09.970
But 64,000 pounds over a lot of oceans.

00:44:09.970 --> 00:44:15.280
And then you get to this question of actual
measurement of impact is different than measuring

00:44:15.280 --> 00:44:16.530
the presence of something.

00:44:16.530 --> 00:44:18.940
So but it's gonna be it's gonna be a fight.

00:44:18.940 --> 00:44:19.940
There will be…

00:44:19.940 --> 00:44:22.510
But it's less likely to have opposition from
the east.

00:44:22.510 --> 00:44:29.860
I think the Eastern culture case for the human
beings and plants and bees and so on and so

00:44:29.860 --> 00:44:30.860
forth.

00:44:30.860 --> 00:44:36.150
I think they, I’m not saying Western isn’t,
but Eastern seems to care for years they have

00:44:36.150 --> 00:44:39.690
been studying conscience and soul and things
like that.

00:44:39.690 --> 00:44:43.620
I think there will be less opposition from
that part of the world wanting to care for

00:44:43.620 --> 00:44:47.100
the planet, you know leave the planet better
than I found it.

00:44:47.100 --> 00:44:51.570
So there may be opposition from some countries,
but as a whole, less.

00:44:51.570 --> 00:44:56.040
But as a manufacturer must be hard because
Europe even on standard chemicals they require

00:44:56.040 --> 00:45:01.060
testing on chemicals that we lead right into
the market right now.

00:45:01.060 --> 00:45:05.840
I think some of the most compelling social
science I've seen on why cultural differences

00:45:05.840 --> 00:45:10.510
are there and this is kind of a catch all
word but sort of intuitively made sense to

00:45:10.510 --> 00:45:11.510
me has to do with trust.

00:45:11.510 --> 00:45:17.270
So how much the public trusts in their governments
ability to regulate in the sciences you know

00:45:17.270 --> 00:45:22.390
in the sort of academic systems’ ability
to be independent.

00:45:22.390 --> 00:45:28.580
And how open the society is are all factors
that kind of govern whether or not the public

00:45:28.580 --> 00:45:35.290
will accept a risk because the reality is
there they are kind of trusting a variety

00:45:35.290 --> 00:45:41.070
of different stakeholders to tell them what
it is and to inform them, right?

00:45:41.070 --> 00:45:49.170
And I think that very in Europe they're really
not very trusting in these kinds of panels

00:45:49.170 --> 00:45:50.170
in Europe that it's it's a very different
kind of feel.

00:45:50.170 --> 00:45:51.700
And that's why we're doing this.

00:45:51.700 --> 00:45:58.930
So if we can you know show not only show how
we're proceeding but also listen to what concerns

00:45:58.930 --> 00:46:04.991
are then not only can we address those but
we can learn something about how to how to

00:46:04.991 --> 00:46:08.791
proceed and you know what more to do.

00:46:08.791 --> 00:46:12.680
It's it's really a conversation even though
you're all sort of blocked by bright lights

00:46:12.680 --> 00:46:14.450
right now.

00:46:14.450 --> 00:46:17.010
You will be part of the conversation, in a
minute we’re going to go to questions shortly.

00:46:17.010 --> 00:46:20.090
But I would say the one thing that I can say
and it's funny when you're a scientist you

00:46:20.090 --> 00:46:24.170
don’t like to have a policy statement because
my job is to create data not to make policy.

00:46:24.170 --> 00:46:27.940
But there is one area that I do feel strongly
that is actually labeling on products and

00:46:27.940 --> 00:46:34.170
I've had a lot of arguments with corporate
folks who say if we give people information

00:46:34.170 --> 00:46:35.520
they'll just get afraid.

00:46:35.520 --> 00:46:38.140
And we don't even know how to label things.

00:46:38.140 --> 00:46:39.450
That second point is true.

00:46:39.450 --> 00:46:41.221
The first point I don't know if I buy.

00:46:41.221 --> 00:46:45.440
But I think that that sort of openness and
saying you know these are the ingredients

00:46:45.440 --> 00:46:51.870
that are in this product and let you the public
have some important insight.

00:46:51.870 --> 00:46:57.500
I think without that there there remains this,
I can't control the risk that I personally

00:46:57.500 --> 00:47:00.810
am assuming, that I think is always going
to be in our way particularly with consumer

00:47:00.810 --> 00:47:01.810
products.

00:47:01.810 --> 00:47:05.270
So I've been pushing for a while on this one
with not a lot of success.

00:47:05.270 --> 00:47:06.940
It’s the one soapbox I have.

00:47:06.940 --> 00:47:12.820
Well this came up in a big way with GMOs and
still the problem was a lot of the labeling

00:47:12.820 --> 00:47:16.810
initiatives that came up at the state level
were were implicitly punitive.

00:47:16.810 --> 00:47:22.120
They were there to add and this is another
this is the question you just sort of made

00:47:22.120 --> 00:47:24.550
the point you made about well what's on the
label.

00:47:24.550 --> 00:47:28.580
Is it just X is here or is it implying this
is a danger.

00:47:28.580 --> 00:47:34.040
I think that there this community because
what we have and the nature of our materials

00:47:34.040 --> 00:47:38.020
I think we could probably if we had a really
good working...people work together.

00:47:38.020 --> 00:47:42.810
I think we could deal with that issue in this
particular setting.

00:47:42.810 --> 00:47:43.810
But I agree.

00:47:43.810 --> 00:47:46.540
I mean is that a reason not to label or is
it a reason just to work really hard to make

00:47:46.540 --> 00:47:51.010
sure the labeling you do is accurate and informative
to the people who need it.

00:47:51.010 --> 00:47:52.010
Or would want it.

00:47:52.010 --> 00:47:55.640
So from my perspective I don't think it fans
the flames but I'm certainly aware there are

00:47:55.640 --> 00:47:57.500
others who think it does.

00:47:57.500 --> 00:48:00.130
So I think that's a that's one really interesting
point.

00:48:00.130 --> 00:48:04.060
And I see very different perspectives in both
the scientific and corporate community in

00:48:04.060 --> 00:48:05.560
different parts of the world.

00:48:05.560 --> 00:48:10.560
By the way another area where this came up
was fracking fluids up to the ground industry

00:48:10.560 --> 00:48:15.650
was resisting saying what was in them and
that led to this whole suspicion factor.

00:48:15.650 --> 00:48:21.850
Then Wyoming a state that's…Wyoming has
actually was one of the first states even

00:48:21.850 --> 00:48:23.020
though it was Cheney's state.

00:48:23.020 --> 00:48:28.650
It's an oil and coal state to move forward
with requirements for open listing and you

00:48:28.650 --> 00:48:32.570
know it's all there for people to explore.

00:48:32.570 --> 00:48:36.100
I'd like to start to think about the questions
and answers.

00:48:36.100 --> 00:48:37.120
This one's for Vicki.

00:48:37.120 --> 00:48:41.440
Do you use sunscreen, if so which kind and
why.

00:48:41.440 --> 00:48:45.810
Consumers Report had a really good we actually
it was one of my favorite collaboration with

00:48:45.810 --> 00:48:46.810
an NGO.

00:48:46.810 --> 00:48:49.930
We worked with Consumers Report on analyzing
sunscreens.

00:48:49.930 --> 00:48:51.580
But this is 2010.

00:48:51.580 --> 00:48:57.230
So I never…I do I have kids, right, they're
teenagers now.

00:48:57.230 --> 00:49:05.400
After doing that work I never use infant sunscreens
don't use anything that says infant.

00:49:05.400 --> 00:49:11.100
And I tend to go for big brands that have
lots like would have a lot of money.

00:49:11.100 --> 00:49:15.460
I can't name them because there are legal
issues but if you come talk to me after my

00:49:15.460 --> 00:49:18.720
talk…the other thing that it caused me to
do because I actually didn't know this data.

00:49:18.720 --> 00:49:23.470
But sunscreen washes off and in fact to get
the full effect of it you really have to reapply

00:49:23.470 --> 00:49:24.470
it a lot.

00:49:24.470 --> 00:49:29.900
So what it actually caused me to do, is when
I do go to the beach or go hiking in the desert

00:49:29.900 --> 00:49:34.110
with my family I actually really into finding
the lightweight clothing and sort of covering

00:49:34.110 --> 00:49:35.980
the body as much as I can.

00:49:35.980 --> 00:49:39.000
And that actually keeps you a little bit cooler
too.

00:49:39.000 --> 00:49:40.310
So my kids you know…

00:49:40.310 --> 00:49:43.210
What a novel idea, like an umbrella?

00:49:43.210 --> 00:49:46.470
Umbrellas go a little too far for my teenage
girl now.

00:49:46.470 --> 00:49:52.280
But I think that they are both you know cover
up a lot and that's actually net safer because

00:49:52.280 --> 00:49:55.931
the reality is if unless you are going to
be putting sunscreen on every 30 minutes you're

00:49:55.931 --> 00:49:58.230
probably going to be losing the benefits anyhow.

00:49:58.230 --> 00:50:00.470
I don't use sunscreen.

00:50:00.470 --> 00:50:02.720
I don't I don't need it.

00:50:02.720 --> 00:50:05.220
And there is an opportunity there.

00:50:05.220 --> 00:50:06.540
Why don't I need it.

00:50:06.540 --> 00:50:12.160
What is the mechanism that prevents the UV
absorption and melanin.

00:50:12.160 --> 00:50:13.160
That's where the opportunity is.

00:50:13.160 --> 00:50:15.550
Getting to the root of biological possibilities.

00:50:15.550 --> 00:50:17.480
I got to ask you though what's the why not
the infant.

00:50:17.480 --> 00:50:18.490
What was the infant issue?

00:50:18.490 --> 00:50:22.960
So you remember I talked about safety by design
so we analyzed and you can read all about

00:50:22.960 --> 00:50:25.160
consumer reports I'm not kidding.

00:50:25.160 --> 00:50:30.460
So we analyzed pigments and we tried to determine
whether they were the peanut M&amp;M types.

00:50:30.460 --> 00:50:36.520
Did they coat their pigments and did they
use zinc oxide which is cheaper and has now

00:50:36.520 --> 00:50:40.590
been found as we identified to be even worse
than the titanium, titanium dioxide.

00:50:40.590 --> 00:50:48.400
That infant sunscreens by and large didn't
coat anything because of a variety of issues

00:50:48.400 --> 00:50:53.780
related to new formulations and you never
want to put a new formulation into the infants.

00:50:53.780 --> 00:50:56.950
You know they want to go with something they've
been using a longer time is how it's explained

00:50:56.950 --> 00:50:57.950
to me.

00:50:57.950 --> 00:51:03.600
So when we did the analysis of anything geared
towards babies we actually found the materials

00:51:03.600 --> 00:51:10.210
they were making ten years ago I was surprised
by that finding but it was pretty consistent

00:51:10.210 --> 00:51:11.210
across the board.

00:51:11.210 --> 00:51:16.500
And when I asked they said oh well anything
infant we we use tried and true in this case

00:51:16.500 --> 00:51:21.000
was actually not really great stuff in my
opinion you know.

00:51:21.000 --> 00:51:24.830
And so in general though I think the other
thing I don't like to do.

00:51:24.830 --> 00:51:29.030
We did some really…this is going to sound
strange, but you can get sunscreens from natural

00:51:29.030 --> 00:51:32.350
sources and sort of the not so standard brands.

00:51:32.350 --> 00:51:36.150
I would not recommend that we saw a lot more
variation.

00:51:36.150 --> 00:51:40.960
So the kinds of pigments you need to purchase
are more expensive and they're harder to formulate.

00:51:40.960 --> 00:51:44.210
So the suspension feels good and it lasts
for a long time.

00:51:44.210 --> 00:51:46.870
The big companies know how to do that better.

00:51:46.870 --> 00:51:49.950
Next question.

00:51:49.950 --> 00:51:56.200
Could you mention the companies that are are
or are likely to benefit from nanotechnology?

00:51:56.200 --> 00:52:00.170
Is that an investment oriented question?

00:52:00.170 --> 00:52:02.600
Wow who won't?

00:52:02.600 --> 00:52:09.430
Large companies such as Monsanto and the BASF
are already using it.

00:52:09.430 --> 00:52:13.780
BASF in fact one of the ones that came up
with the brush that you can use to remove

00:52:13.780 --> 00:52:18.800
dust, but then they had to back off when the
nanotoxicity question came up and the companies

00:52:18.800 --> 00:52:25.520
which make skateboards and so on to make make
it make their skateboard so much better that

00:52:25.520 --> 00:52:27.010
the less friction they benefit.

00:52:27.010 --> 00:52:32.150
What I would say in the future probably the
small startup companies like the one I mentioned

00:52:32.150 --> 00:52:38.610
probably has more opportunities to make new
products and so on.

00:52:38.610 --> 00:52:47.320
So already BASF…Benz cars maybe I shouldn't
promote any company but Benz companies they

00:52:47.320 --> 00:52:48.670
have scratch resistant.

00:52:48.670 --> 00:52:53.290
And also it glows very nicely.

00:52:53.290 --> 00:53:00.080
So those companies are already using nanotechnology
for better purpose, there are many others.

00:53:00.080 --> 00:53:06.120
Drug companies of course many drug companies
are using for delivery of delivery of drugs.

00:53:06.120 --> 00:53:09.880
In medicine it's the little companies that
are doing exploration and they're going to

00:53:09.880 --> 00:53:17.970
get purchased by larger companies, so the
ones that have moved forward are into trials.

00:53:17.970 --> 00:53:19.890
You can keep an eye on in terms of therapies.

00:53:19.890 --> 00:53:25.680
There are tremendous moves forward in diagnostics,
energy storage companies, you know whoever

00:53:25.680 --> 00:53:32.720
makes the more efficient faster charging larger
storage battery it's going to.

00:53:32.720 --> 00:53:37.720
Going to be there for the people who want
electric cars and homes storage, it's an enormous

00:53:37.720 --> 00:53:38.720
potential market.

00:53:38.720 --> 00:53:41.870
I think that the question is which companies
won't, but.

00:53:41.870 --> 00:53:46.890
One of the sectors that when I lived in Houston
for many years has really adopted, it's actually

00:53:46.890 --> 00:53:48.620
gotten much more R and D oriented.

00:53:48.620 --> 00:53:52.310
Is actually the oil and gas, the traditional
oil and gas companies.

00:53:52.310 --> 00:53:56.000
And what really woke them up was actually
the development of natural gas and fracking

00:53:56.000 --> 00:53:58.330
technology because it really shifted their
dynamic.

00:53:58.330 --> 00:54:03.610
And it was fundamentally a technology break,
not a breakthrough but a sort of coordinated

00:54:03.610 --> 00:54:05.630
thing that happened that made it possible.

00:54:05.630 --> 00:54:12.210
And ever since about 2007, 2008 they've taken
a much more aggressive stance looking at incorporation

00:54:12.210 --> 00:54:18.040
of new materials and you know everything from
I.T. to nano and to everything that they do.

00:54:18.040 --> 00:54:22.520
So the efficiency particularly I think in
the natural gas areas both for storage and

00:54:22.520 --> 00:54:26.790
extraction is something that those companies
are strongly focused on, because they now

00:54:26.790 --> 00:54:31.120
see that it can really benefit their bottom
line which they didn't see that 10 years earlier.

00:54:31.120 --> 00:54:35.060
They were kind of stuck in their ways and
just making money pumping stuff out of the

00:54:35.060 --> 00:54:36.840
ground for such a long time.

00:54:36.840 --> 00:54:41.190
It was fracking that woke them up because
really up until then it was like exploration.

00:54:41.190 --> 00:54:45.050
Could you identify where there was the most
oil and if you were better at doing that than

00:54:45.050 --> 00:54:47.990
your competitor you were more likely to make
the best bet.

00:54:47.990 --> 00:54:53.390
Now it's kind of the Wild West in the very
different forms of carbon based energy that

00:54:53.390 --> 00:54:54.390
are available.

00:54:54.390 --> 00:54:56.240
I'm not saying it's a good or bad thing, but
it exists.

00:54:56.240 --> 00:55:01.150
So if it's going to exist it's good to make
it more efficient and allow them to extract

00:55:01.150 --> 00:55:03.530
cleaner fuels more efficiently.

00:55:03.530 --> 00:55:10.190
So I think that, that that sector woke up
pretty recently and continues to invest heavily.

00:55:10.190 --> 00:55:13.060
Primarily in efficiency of extraction.

00:55:13.060 --> 00:55:15.920
They've always and you know maybe there'll
be a breakthrough in exploration based on

00:55:15.920 --> 00:55:16.920
nanotech.

00:55:16.920 --> 00:55:17.920
Oh yeah catalysis.

00:55:17.920 --> 00:55:22.530
It has been there has been there for decades.

00:55:22.530 --> 00:55:27.860
And you know one of the innovations I particularly
like is the idea of using nano porous materials

00:55:27.860 --> 00:55:33.200
in part because the guy at Mobile who did
it has the same name as me, Paul Weiss, who

00:55:33.200 --> 00:55:36.980
was my colleague back when I was at Penn State.

00:55:36.980 --> 00:55:41.870
But that dramatically increase the efficiency
of oil refining in the energy content that

00:55:41.870 --> 00:55:44.440
we could get out of what we took out of the
ground.

00:55:44.440 --> 00:55:51.991
And so those advances preceded what much of
what we call the field of nanoscience and

00:55:51.991 --> 00:55:52.991
nanotechnology.

00:55:52.991 --> 00:55:58.890
After the oil spill in the Gulf of Mexico
they used some material which some said toxic,

00:55:58.890 --> 00:56:01.930
the company which made it said it’s not
toxic.

00:56:01.930 --> 00:56:09.510
Then there was an opportunity for companies
to make again proteins bio proteins, which

00:56:09.510 --> 00:56:15.420
will coat the oil particles in such a way
that they will stay in nano form for a long

00:56:15.420 --> 00:56:16.420
time.

00:56:16.420 --> 00:56:17.420
And why is it good?

00:56:17.420 --> 00:56:20.300
Because microbes have an opportunity to eat
that oil.

00:56:20.300 --> 00:56:30.170
So this kind of nano films of oil droplets
had an opportunity, which some companies used.

00:56:30.170 --> 00:56:33.140
That's where I got into the Stephen Hawking’s
episode of Brave New World.

00:56:33.140 --> 00:56:40.970
Oh interesting, you mean so when the oil is
in smaller amounts it's easier for the microbes

00:56:40.970 --> 00:56:42.040
to break it down?

00:56:42.040 --> 00:56:44.990
There are many ways by which they burn up
the oil.

00:56:44.990 --> 00:56:45.990
That's one way.

00:56:45.990 --> 00:56:46.990
There are many ways.

00:56:46.990 --> 00:56:50.180
One way is to use the microbes to eat the
oil.

00:56:50.180 --> 00:56:55.190
Well for microbes to eat the oil there has
to be a lot of surface area of oil droplets.

00:56:55.190 --> 00:56:58.310
For that you have to have the oil droplets
in nano form.

00:56:58.310 --> 00:57:06.400
So what you do is this biosurfactants…will
coat the nanoparticles and the film is so

00:57:06.400 --> 00:57:12.400
robust that it will stay as an emulsion droplet
for a long time and the microbes can eat it.

00:57:12.400 --> 00:57:13.540
Another question.

00:57:13.540 --> 00:57:19.240
There was a comment earlier about distinction
between basic fundamental research and how

00:57:19.240 --> 00:57:23.480
you mentioned that some of the problems that
you thought your field faced actually required

00:57:23.480 --> 00:57:29.470
that in the current climate with sort of potentially
not conducive to that…I was hoping you could

00:57:29.470 --> 00:57:34.840
expand a little bit more about whether you
think that's specific to your field or in

00:57:34.840 --> 00:57:40.570
broader science and why you think that is
and just sort of your general comments on

00:57:40.570 --> 00:57:41.570
that front.

00:57:41.570 --> 00:57:42.570
Yeah.

00:57:42.570 --> 00:57:43.570
It's a it's a really hard one.

00:57:43.570 --> 00:57:48.780
So I think that since I've been it's it's
a double edged sword.

00:57:48.780 --> 00:57:52.500
I would I would take some responsibility as
a scientist for this situation.

00:57:52.500 --> 00:57:56.590
So you know back in the 70s there was Sputnik
and a lot of these agencies that are funding

00:57:56.590 --> 00:58:01.170
us now got started with this sort of I call
it the fear the fear of Russia beating us

00:58:01.170 --> 00:58:06.590
and then it sort of shifted to a hope argument
by investing in science and technology we

00:58:06.590 --> 00:58:08.870
can have a better world a better economy more
jobs.

00:58:08.870 --> 00:58:15.060
And that was sort of the different ways in
which we decide to fund what we do and I think

00:58:15.060 --> 00:58:20.590
in our quest to make what we do more impactful,
so to link what we do in the lab which is

00:58:20.590 --> 00:58:27.620
a fairly abstract detailed kind of work and
to make it relevant we're driven to make applications,

00:58:27.620 --> 00:58:28.620
right?

00:58:28.620 --> 00:58:32.670
We want to show that I didn't just say that
hey I could use my nanoparticles as an antioxidant

00:58:32.670 --> 00:58:36.900
and use them in medicine some day I am going
to show you that.

00:58:36.900 --> 00:58:44.770
And what I think that tilted is it made it
extraordinarily difficult to put forward much

00:58:44.770 --> 00:58:52.090
more difficult to find agencies and programs
willing to do the solely fundamental…I would

00:58:52.090 --> 00:58:57.300
call it I hate to use the word sort of trooper
but that the fabric of science is really the

00:58:57.300 --> 00:59:03.710
repetition that people checking, and that's
harder to do now, you have to always focus

00:59:03.710 --> 00:59:07.560
on the application and then you have to deliver
on that application and that's what you promised

00:59:07.560 --> 00:59:13.540
and guess what that means you spend less time
systematically unraveling underlying mechanisms.

00:59:13.540 --> 00:59:18.950
And I think that like I said I always I hate
to blame other people, I think as a scientist

00:59:18.950 --> 00:59:23.920
I'm sort of guilty of it because I wanted
to you know in my career make things relevant.

00:59:23.920 --> 00:59:26.740
And I think that's shifted the field and I
think nano has been part of that.

00:59:26.740 --> 00:59:31.270
I would also say the climate of course is
changing for that and certainly the current

00:59:31.270 --> 00:59:36.960
administration my early reads from my network
are not positive for fundamental science.

00:59:36.960 --> 00:59:40.690
You know anything that I'm going to write
a proposal on is got to be I'm going to either

00:59:40.690 --> 00:59:43.710
partner with a corporation or I'm going to
start a company or I'm going to.

00:59:43.710 --> 00:59:47.750
So I don't have time and I don't have that
ability unless I can couch it and kind of

00:59:47.750 --> 00:59:49.150
do it in drips and drabs.

00:59:49.150 --> 00:59:51.200
So for me I find that more difficult.

00:59:51.200 --> 00:59:53.250
I don't know if I'm the only one.

00:59:53.250 --> 00:59:56.740
So we're close to wrapping up I want to give
you the opportunity to come up with a closing

00:59:56.740 --> 01:00:00.420
thought but my my question for you and my
prompt for you going forward is as I said

01:00:00.420 --> 01:00:05.760
at the beginning we seemed to be in a race
between potency and self awareness, between

01:00:05.760 --> 01:00:08.060
what we can do and what we choose to do.

01:00:08.060 --> 01:00:13.280
And I don't know going forward do you feel
we're on the right track to get that answered

01:00:13.280 --> 01:00:18.640
in a way that people 100 years from now will
say, oh they kind of worked it out.

01:00:18.640 --> 01:00:22.410
So I think you know as you heard one of the
advantages of the community is we bring in

01:00:22.410 --> 01:00:26.830
people from other fields we're naturally curious
we talk across those fields and I think that

01:00:26.830 --> 01:00:32.320
bodes well for looking at the consequences
and trying to explore those in advance and

01:00:32.320 --> 01:00:38.580
engaging with the broader community as well,
in what the issues are how to proceed what

01:00:38.580 --> 01:00:41.320
to develop.

01:00:41.320 --> 01:00:45.460
What information you know we want to make
sure everybody has.

01:00:45.460 --> 01:00:52.950
And so, you know I feel really a positive
outlook for the development of the technology

01:00:52.950 --> 01:01:00.870
and in a safe way just because of that stance
really of the people in the field and where

01:01:00.870 --> 01:01:02.760
we came from and how we interact.

01:01:02.760 --> 01:01:03.760
Great.

